    <p style="font-size:13px;color:var(--text-light);margin-bottom:24px;">Five data-driven recommendations for category management, pricing optimization, and customer experience improvement. Each recommendation is directly supported by findings from the analysis above.</p>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-number">1</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <h4>Restructure Deep-Discount Promotions — Cap Aggressive Discounts for Quality-Sensitive SKUs</h4>
        <p>The data shows a clear inverse relationship (r = &minus;0.155) between discount depth and customer rating. Products discounted 81–100% average just 3.98 stars, versus 4.15 for those under 20% off. Deep discounts are eroding the perception of product quality without driving meaningful engagement gains (discount–volume corr. = +0.012).</p>
        <span class="rec-evidence">Data: 81–100% bucket avg rating 3.98; 0–20% bucket avg rating 4.15; 439 products currently exceed 60% discount</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-number">2</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <h4>Invest in Home&amp;Kitchen Category Quality — Close the Gap vs. Peers</h4>
        <p>Home&amp;Kitchen (448 products, 30.6% of catalog) trails both Electronics and Computers&amp;Accessories in average rating (4.04 vs. 4.08–4.15) and engagement (avg 6,676 reviews vs. 17,137–29,998). The Heating, Cooling &amp; Air Quality sub-category is the weakest performer at 3.99. A targeted assortment audit to remove or improve low-rated SKUs would lift the category average and reduce return-driven costs.</p>
        <span class="rec-evidence">Data: Home&amp;Kitchen avg rating 4.04 vs. Electronics 4.08, C&amp;A 4.15; Heating sub-cat avg 3.99; avg rating count 6,676 vs. category peers</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-number">3</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <h4>Address Product Reliability as the #1 Customer Complaint</h4>
        <p>Review content analysis shows 261 combined mentions of "not working," "defective," and "broken" — the single largest cluster of negative feedback. Combined with 265 damage/return mentions, product failure accounts for the majority of negative customer experience. Implementing stricter pre-listing quality testing and enforcing seller warranty compliance would directly address the pain driving the catalog's 4.7% sub-3.5 rating tail.</p>
        <span class="rec-evidence">Data: "not working" 149x, "stopped" 108x, "defective" 63x in review content; 68 products rated below 3.5</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <div class="rec-number">4</div>
      <div class="rec-body">
        <h4>Implement Catalog Hygiene Controls for Counterfeit &amp; Low-Quality Listings</h4>
        <p>The word "fake" appears 21 times in customer reviews — a small but significant signal of counterfeit product listings. Combined with 288 "bad" mentions and 47 "worst" mentions, there is a subset of catalog listings actively harming brand trust. A systematic review of products rated below 3.5 (68 products) with cross-referencing of counterfeit-flagged reviews would identify the highest-priority removals.</p>
        <span class="rec-evidence">Data: "fake" 21x in reviews; "bad" 288x; 68 products rated &lt;3.5; 1 product rated below 2.0</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
